    "data_types": [
      "teleseismic"
    ],
    "method": "Non-negative least-squares kinematic finite-fault inversion of teleseismic P waves from 30 stations (30-90 degrees), using multiple rupture time windows, smoothing, and generalised-ray synthetic waveforms; NORTH-dipping alternative from Figure 5, Table 3.",
    "green_function_model": "Generalized ray theory synthetics with the one-dimensional velocity model in Table 2 of Papadopoulos et al. (2019).",
    "slip_type": "variable",
    "smoothing_constraint": true,
    "notes": "NORTH-DIPPING KINEMATIC FINITE-FAULT ALTERNATIVE (Figure 5, TOTAL SLIP), not the south-dipping preferred solution (Figure 4) and not the separate InSAR static model (Table 4). Data are figure-digitized scalar slip values, NOT released original inversion coefficients. The 18 along-strike by 6 down-dip mesh is explicitly reported (108 subfaults). Fault length 27 km and model bottom depth 15 km, strike 275 deg, dip 39 deg, nucleation depth 9 km, rupture velocity 3.2 km/s and 0.3 s rise time are the NORTH-DIPPING values from Table 3. With top edge assumed at vertical depth 0 km, inferred down-dip width is about 15/sin(39 deg)=23.83 km, rounded to 24 km, or 4 km along-dip per row; cell depths reflect that planar convention. The original figure vertical coordinate ranges 0 to -15 km (depth on the fault-plane cross-section); negative ticks are plotted downward, but output depth_km is POSITIVE. Color calibration uses the 0-2.4 m colorbar in the extracted high-resolution Figure 5 embedded raster. Red first-week aftershock dots, black 0.1-m contour labels, hypocenter star, colored annotations, and dark line work were masked where detectable before reconstructing each 18x6 cell from a robust median of remaining calibrated colors. Cell-by-cell QA and original-vs-reconstruction diagnostic image are supplied separately. Digitization cannot perfectly separate all overlain contours and aftershocks; cells affected by heavy overlays or pale low-slip contours are less reliable. The north-dipping solution has a paper-reported peak of about 2.2 m, which need not equal the peak after patch-center sampling. Patch rake -78 deg is the Harvard CMT NP1 initial nodal-plane rake discussed in Section 2, not an explicit map of spatially variable final inversion rake. The separate InSAR north solution fixed rake -77 deg (Table 4); do NOT conflate it with this kinematic map. Relocated mainshock hypocenter is latitude 36.960 N longitude 27.410 E depth 8.5 km in Section 3, distinct from the 9 km rupture start in the north-dipping inversion. The paper does not provide absolute geographic origins for each figure grid vertex; patch centroid geographic coordinates therefore remain null. The map is displayed from west to east while strike 275 degrees points approximately westward; along_strike_km in this JSON follows Figure 5 left-to-right coordinate, and is NOT strike-azimuth-signed; interpret as plot-local fault distance. The reference-point fields are null; do not project patch coordinates into geography without independent georeferencing. The north-dipping option is an explicitly explored alternative, whereas the authors prefer the south-dipping fault using multi-dataset evidence. Summary mean is figure-digitized mean across 108 modeled patches, including zero slip, not an independently reported mean."
  },
